1
00:00:03,550 --> 00:00:08,149
Search for a part number with an embedding
model, and the exact token can get

2
00:00:08,149 --> 00:00:08,925
blurred away.

3
00:00:09,550 --> 00:00:14,442
Search for "automobile" with keyword search,
and you miss every document that says

4
00:00:14,442 --> 00:00:14,800
"car".

5
00:00:15,100 --> 00:00:19,975
In this video, we'll look at when to use each
retriever, and how to combine them.

6
00:00:20,983 --> 00:00:22,783
Here's the core idea.

7
00:00:23,083 --> 00:00:30,058
BM25 wins on exact tokens: identifiers,
rare terms, names and error codes.

8
00:00:30,358 --> 00:00:35,883
Dense retrieval wins on paraphrase, when queries
and documents use different words.

9
00:00:36,183 --> 00:00:39,058
Their errors are largely uncorrelated.

10
00:00:39,683 --> 00:00:43,983
So for almost any production system,
the default is hybrid.

11
00:00:44,283 --> 00:00:49,558
Run both retrievers, fuse the lists with
reciprocal rank fusion, and rerank.

12
00:00:49,858 --> 00:00:54,924
But decide by running all three on a labeled
set from your own traffic, because

13
00:00:54,924 --> 00:00:56,783
rankings flip across domains.

14
00:00:57,783 --> 00:01:00,708
Before choosing, pin down a few things.

15
00:01:01,008 --> 00:01:03,558
First, what do the queries look like?

16
00:01:03,858 --> 00:01:08,883
Two or three keywords, IDs and SKUs, or
natural-language questions?

17
00:01:09,183 --> 00:01:14,483
The more queries look like identifiers,
the more weight BM25 deserves.

18
00:01:15,083 --> 00:01:18,358
Second, how specialized is the vocabulary?

19
00:01:18,658 --> 00:01:24,508
Biomedical, legal, code or internal acronyms
all stress off-the-shelf embedders.

20
00:01:24,808 --> 00:01:30,544
And is there in-domain data, like click logs or
labeled pairs, to fine-tune a dense

21
00:01:30,544 --> 00:01:30,958
model?

22
00:01:31,583 --> 00:01:34,383
Third, is the corpus multilingual?

23
00:01:34,683 --> 00:01:39,835
Cross-lingual retrieval is where dense models
have a structural advantage over

24
00:01:39,835 --> 00:01:40,958
lexical matching.

25
00:01:41,583 --> 00:01:45,258
And fourth, latency, cost, and
the existing stack.

26
00:01:45,558 --> 00:01:50,408
A second index is cheap in milliseconds,
but not free in operations.

27
00:01:50,708 --> 00:01:57,272
If you already run Elasticsearch, OpenSearch or
Vespa, adding BM25 or vectors next

28
00:01:57,272 --> 00:01:59,433
to it is often nearly free.

29
00:02:00,450 --> 00:02:02,825
Start with BM25.

30
00:02:03,125 --> 00:02:09,021
It scores a document by term frequency, with
saturation, so each extra repeat of a

31
00:02:09,021 --> 00:02:10,100
word adds less.

32
00:02:10,717 --> 00:02:13,217
It normalizes for document length.

33
00:02:13,817 --> 00:02:18,492
And it uses inverse document frequency,
which rewards rare terms.

34
00:02:19,517 --> 00:02:24,367
That gives BM25 exact match and
rare-term precision.

35
00:02:24,667 --> 00:02:26,817
Its scoring is transparent.

36
00:02:27,117 --> 00:02:32,417
It's cheap: an inverted index over
100M documents is routine.

37
00:02:32,717 --> 00:02:36,692
It needs zero training, and it holds
up under domain shift.

38
00:02:37,317 --> 00:02:43,081
Its weaknesses are vocabulary mismatch, car
versus automobile, no understanding of

39
00:02:43,081 --> 00:02:48,142
negation or intent, and weak results on
long natural-language questions.

40
00:02:49,150 --> 00:02:52,100
Dense retrieval uses a bi-encoder.

41
00:02:52,400 --> 00:02:57,645
It embeds the query and each document
independently, then ranks by cosine or

42
00:02:57,645 --> 00:03:02,200
dot-product similarity over an
approximate nearest neighbor index.

43
00:03:02,817 --> 00:03:08,067
It handles synonyms, paraphrase, cross-lingual
matching and conceptual queries.

44
00:03:08,683 --> 00:03:13,358
But exact rare tokens get blurred: part
numbers, names and codes.

45
00:03:13,983 --> 00:03:18,033
Quality degrades out of domain, and
its failures are opaque.

46
00:03:18,333 --> 00:03:24,641
On specialized corpora, off-the-shelf models
can underperform BM25 until they're

47
00:03:24,641 --> 00:03:25,508
fine-tuned.

48
00:03:26,117 --> 00:03:28,892
And vectors cost compute and storage.

49
00:03:29,192 --> 00:03:33,042
Every model change means
re-embedding the whole corpus.

50
00:03:34,050 --> 00:03:36,550
The picture has shifted over time.

51
00:03:36,850 --> 00:03:43,181
In 2021, the BEIR benchmark showed BM25
beating many dense models zero-shot, on

52
00:03:43,181 --> 00:03:45,825
domains they weren't trained for.

53
00:03:46,450 --> 00:03:53,583
Then embedders trained on large, diverse,
weakly supervised data, starting with E5

54
00:03:53,583 --> 00:03:56,975
in 2022, beat BM25 on the BEIR average.

55
00:03:57,275 --> 00:04:01,050
That's now the norm for strong
general-purpose embedders.

56
00:04:01,350 --> 00:04:07,750
But BM25 still wins on individual datasets,
and on identifier-heavy queries.

57
00:04:08,050 --> 00:04:10,875
So the average alone doesn't
settle the choice.

58
00:04:11,883 --> 00:04:14,433
Two more options sit between these.

59
00:04:14,733 --> 00:04:19,700
Learned sparse retrieval, like SPLADE, has a
model predict term weights and

60
00:04:19,700 --> 00:04:23,408
expansion terms, served from an
ordinary inverted index.

61
00:04:23,708 --> 00:04:30,000
It often approaches dense quality in-domain,
while keeping BM25's interpretability

62
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:31,458
and exact matching.

63
00:04:32,083 --> 00:04:37,991
Late interaction, like ColBERT, keeps one vector
per token, and matches query tokens

64
00:04:37,991 --> 00:04:40,733
against document tokens at search time.

65
00:04:41,033 --> 00:04:44,308
Quality and out-of-domain
behavior are strong.

66
00:04:44,608 --> 00:04:49,807
But storing a vector per token costs an
order of magnitude or more storage,

67
00:04:49,807 --> 00:04:50,708
uncompressed.

68
00:04:51,008 --> 00:04:57,637
ColBERTv2's compression cuts that 6-10×, and the
PLAID engine brings search down to

69
00:04:57,637 --> 00:05:00,033
tens of milliseconds on a GPU.

70
00:05:01,050 --> 00:05:03,700
Now, hybrid retrieval done right.

71
00:05:04,000 --> 00:05:09,610
Here's the whole pipeline: two retrievers in
parallel, rank-based fusion, then a

72
00:05:09,610 --> 00:05:12,275
cross-encoder reranker before the LLM.

73
00:05:12,883 --> 00:05:14,408
Step one.

74
00:05:14,708 --> 00:05:20,108
Run BM25 top 100 and dense top
100 concurrently.

75
00:05:20,408 --> 00:05:26,333
Combined latency is about the maximum of
the two, typically 10-50 ms.

76
00:05:26,633 --> 00:05:30,983
So hybrid adds an index to operate,
but very little latency.

77
00:05:31,583 --> 00:05:33,108
Step two.

78
00:05:33,408 --> 00:05:36,708
Fuse the two lists with
reciprocal rank fusion.

79
00:05:37,717 --> 00:05:44,442
For each document, RRF sums one over k plus its
rank in each list, with k around 60.

80
00:05:45,050 --> 00:05:46,975
Here's a worked example.

81
00:05:47,275 --> 00:05:54,625
A document ranked first by BM25 and fifth
by dense scores about 0.0318.

82
00:05:54,925 --> 00:06:00,575
A document ranked second by dense
only scores about 0.0161.

83
00:06:00,875 --> 00:06:04,475
Agreement between retrievers
beats a single high rank.

84
00:06:05,083 --> 00:06:10,408
RRF uses ranks, not raw scores, so it
needs no score normalization.

85
00:06:10,708 --> 00:06:12,833
That makes it the right default.

86
00:06:13,133 --> 00:06:18,442
A weighted blend of normalized scores can beat
it, if you tune the weight on labeled

87
00:06:18,442 --> 00:06:18,758
data.

88
00:06:19,058 --> 00:06:24,308
But that blend is brittle when score
distributions drift after a model change.

89
00:06:25,317 --> 00:06:26,867
Step three.

90
00:06:27,167 --> 00:06:32,997
Rerank the fused top 100-200 with a
cross-encoder, which scores each query and

91
00:06:32,997 --> 00:06:34,567
passage pair jointly.

92
00:06:35,183 --> 00:06:39,108
And step four, optionally route or
weight by query type.

93
00:06:39,408 --> 00:06:45,812
Queries that look like identifiers, such as
codes or quoted strings, get more BM25

94
00:06:45,812 --> 00:06:46,358
weight.

95
00:06:46,658 --> 00:06:50,308
Long natural-language questions
get more dense weight.

96
00:06:51,317 --> 00:06:52,917
Is it worth it?

97
00:06:53,217 --> 00:06:57,717
Anthropic's contextual retrieval
experiments show the gains stack.

98
00:06:58,017 --> 00:07:04,142
Measured as the top-20 retrieval failure
rate, the baseline was 5.7%.

99
00:07:04,750 --> 00:07:11,425
With contextual embeddings, it fell to
3.7%, 35% fewer failures.

100
00:07:12,050 --> 00:07:18,700
Adding contextual BM25 took it
to 2.9%, 49% fewer.

101
00:07:19,317 --> 00:07:25,942
And adding reranking on top brought it
to 1.9%, 67% fewer failures.

102
00:07:26,242 --> 00:07:32,092
In that setup, reranking took the top 150
fused candidates down to 20.

103
00:07:33,117 --> 00:07:34,967
So how do you choose?

104
00:07:35,267 --> 00:07:41,448
Use BM25 only on a cold start with no labeled
data, for identifier-heavy queries

105
00:07:41,448 --> 00:07:47,708
like log search, code symbols, legal citations
and SKUs, or under strict cost and

106
00:07:47,708 --> 00:07:48,867
latency limits.

107
00:07:49,167 --> 00:07:51,692
And always use it as the baseline.

108
00:07:51,992 --> 00:07:58,226
If your dense system doesn't beat BM25 on your
eval set, stop and investigate before

109
00:07:58,226 --> 00:07:59,117
shipping it.

110
00:07:59,717 --> 00:08:04,880
Use dense only for short-text semantic
matching, like FAQ deduplication or

111
00:08:04,880 --> 00:08:10,672
support-ticket routing, for cross-lingual
retrieval, and for conversational queries

112
00:08:10,672 --> 00:08:12,417
with low lexical overlap.

113
00:08:12,717 --> 00:08:18,792
Even then, prove on your eval set that BM25
adds nothing before you drop it.

114
00:08:19,417 --> 00:08:24,785
And hybrid is the default for general
enterprise RAG, for mixed query populations,

115
00:08:24,785 --> 00:08:28,517
and anywhere recall matters more
than a few milliseconds.

116
00:08:29,517 --> 00:08:35,763
To measure it, sample 500-2,000 queries from
real traffic, stratified by type: ID

117
00:08:35,763 --> 00:08:40,467
lookups, keywords, natural-language
questions, and multi-hop.

118
00:08:41,083 --> 00:08:43,033
Use pooled judgments.

119
00:08:43,333 --> 00:08:49,133
Label the union of the top 20 from every system
under test, so the eval isn't biased

120
00:08:49,133 --> 00:08:50,583
toward the incumbent.

121
00:08:51,183 --> 00:08:55,858
For the first stage, report recall
at 100 and recall at 50.

122
00:08:56,158 --> 00:09:00,108
The reranker can only reorder what
the first stage gives it.

123
00:09:00,717 --> 00:09:06,092
For the final list, report nDCG at 10
and MRR after reranking.

124
00:09:07,117 --> 00:09:09,642
Always report metrics per slice.

125
00:09:09,942 --> 00:09:15,282
The hybrid gain usually concentrates in the
slice where one retriever collapses, and

126
00:09:15,282 --> 00:09:16,617
the average hides it.

127
00:09:17,217 --> 00:09:22,267
Compare the overlap between the
BM25 and dense top 50.

128
00:09:22,567 --> 00:09:27,567
As a rule of thumb, at 80% or more
overlap, hybrid adds little.

129
00:09:27,867 --> 00:09:33,292
At 30-50% overlap, fusion typically
adds several points of recall.

130
00:09:33,917 --> 00:09:39,213
Use a paired bootstrap or permutation test
over queries, and report confidence

131
00:09:39,213 --> 00:09:39,892
intervals.

132
00:09:40,192 --> 00:09:45,819
Online, track click-through on citations,
answer acceptance, and how often users

133
00:09:45,819 --> 00:09:47,367
reformulate the query.

134
00:09:48,383 --> 00:09:50,833
Here are the tradeoffs side by side.

135
00:09:51,133 --> 00:09:57,883
BM25 is exact, cheap and interpretable, but
suffers from vocabulary mismatch.

136
00:09:58,483 --> 00:10:04,399
A dense bi-encoder is semantic and multilingual,
but misses rare tokens and costs a

137
00:10:04,399 --> 00:10:06,608
re-embed on every model change.

138
00:10:07,217 --> 00:10:13,292
Hybrid with RRF and a reranker has the best
recall across slices, at the price of

139
00:10:13,292 --> 00:10:15,017
two indexes to operate.

140
00:10:15,317 --> 00:10:18,217
RRF is robust with zero tuning.

141
00:10:18,517 --> 00:10:23,983
A tuned linear blend can win by a point or a
few, but needs labels and re-tuning

142
00:10:23,983 --> 00:10:25,692
whenever a model changes.

143
00:10:26,717 --> 00:10:29,042
Finally, the common mistakes.

144
00:10:29,342 --> 00:10:33,267
Claiming dense is strictly
better than BM25.

145
00:10:33,567 --> 00:10:40,317
BM25 still wins on identifier-heavy slices, so
dropping it loses those queries.

146
00:10:40,917 --> 00:10:45,467
Adding raw BM25 scores to
cosine similarities.

147
00:10:45,767 --> 00:10:50,367
The scales are incompatible, so one
retriever silently dominates.

148
00:10:50,667 --> 00:10:54,192
Use RRF, or calibrated normalization.

149
00:10:54,817 --> 00:10:59,067
Evaluating on a public benchmark
instead of your own queries.

150
00:10:59,367 --> 00:11:02,642
You end up optimizing for
someone else's traffic.

151
00:11:03,250 --> 00:11:04,975
Pooling bias.

152
00:11:05,275 --> 00:11:10,360
If you label only the incumbent's results,
the new retriever's finds count as

153
00:11:10,360 --> 00:11:13,200
non-relevant, so better
systems look worse.

154
00:11:13,817 --> 00:11:18,517
And inconsistent ACLs and deletes
across the two indexes.

155
00:11:18,817 --> 00:11:23,292
A document deleted or restricted in one
index leaks through the other.

156
00:11:24,317 --> 00:11:25,992
So, to recap.

157
00:11:26,292 --> 00:11:29,217
Keep BM25 as the baseline.

158
00:11:29,517 --> 00:11:33,367
Make hybrid, with RRF and a
reranker, the default.

159
00:11:33,667 --> 00:11:38,780
And choose on your own traffic, slice by slice,
because that's where the differences

160
00:11:38,780 --> 00:11:39,267
show up.

161
00:11:40,283 --> 00:11:43,808
The full article, with every
source, is on vidyal.ai.

162
00:11:44,817 --> 00:11:46,592
Thanks for watching.

163
00:11:46,892 --> 00:11:50,767
Next in the series, adding a
reranker, and proving it helped.

164
00:11:51,067 --> 00:11:53,192
Subscribe so you don't miss it.
